  "body": "Found by the independent review of PR #60 (#19); the gap predates that PR.\n\nTurning ops fit their spindle from the op's facets (`Frame::about`, any position, lean allowed up to ~89 deg, `LEAN_MIN`). The Fanuc lathe post (`assets/posts/fanuc-lathe.cps` ~96-105) computes radius from the work-offset origin (orgX/orgY) and takes Z as world Z - it assumes a vertical spindle through the work-offset origin.\n\nWhen the fitted axis is off that line the post mostly refuses (\"off the spindle axis meridian\"), but not always: a model whose axis leans a few degrees in Y with its X on the work-offset origin gives radial = (0, cos, -sin), every move has dx = 0, and the post accepts it, writing diameters and Z as if the axis were vertical. The machine cuts a taper error - wrong output with no warning.\n\nDone means: a turning op whose fitted axis is not the machine spindle (world Z through the work-offset origin, within a stated tolerance) is refused by name at generation (Validate early), or the program is transformed so the posted diameters/Z are the part's; pinned by a test with a few-degree Y-lean that today posts silently. The field simulation (#19 / PR #60) must agree with whatever the post emits.\n",
